  "query_preview": "..\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\n\nThe main forecast challenge for Sunday looks to be convective\npotential for the eastern zones along the MT/ND border this\nafternoon and evening as a shortwave flattens the ridge. HREF max\nensemble wind gust and updraft helicity guidance as well as \nensemble 40dbz reflectivity all show consensus on what is expected\nto unfold. Storms that develop will track southeast from Canada \ninto Daniels, Sheridan, Eastern Roosevelt, Richland, and Wibaux \nCounties before pushing into North Dakota. Timing looks to be \nbetween about 3PM and 8PM on the Montana side. A few of these \nstorms may be strong to severe with damaging wind gusts to 60 mph \nwith hail as a secondary threat.\n\nOf note, the SPC D1 outlook carries a Marginal Risk for severe\nthunderstorms in our eastern zones with a slight risk right up\nagainst the MT/ND border.\n \nZonal flow with various shortwaves look to continue at times \nthrough the remainder of the week as a trough begins to set up \nover the Pacific Northwest. This will lead to cooler temperatures \nbut more active chances for showers and thunderstorms each day.\nEnsemble guidance has trended higher for the QPF, notably for the\nsecond half of the week. Probabilities are increasing that much of\nNE Montana will see beneficial rainfall during this time. As this\nis further out in the forecast period, days 5 through 7, will\nawait run to run continuity before having much confidence in the\ndetails, but this will be the next period worth focusing in on.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nPops were adjusted near term in collaboration with neighbors and\nto better reflect the expected convective evolution for Sunday\nafternoon and evening. Confidence is growing on an isolated\nsevere threat this afternoon and evening across the eastern zones\nas suggested by excellent high resolution model consensus.\nConfidence also continues to emerge toward a wetter and more\nactive pattern this week, especially for the second half.",
